---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Restaurant",
  name: "The Palm Court at The Plaza",
  servesCuisine: ["Afternoon Tea", "American", "Contemporary"],
  priceRange: "$$$$",
  telephone: "+1-212-546-5300",
  url: "https://www.theplazany.com/dining/the-palm-court/",
  address: {
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    streetAddress: "768 5th Ave",
    addressLocality: "New York",
    addressRegion: "NY",
    postalCode: "10019",
    addressCountry: "US",
  },
  description:
    "Iconic afternoon tea room under a stained-glass dome at The Plaza hotel on Fifth Avenue, New York.",
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Palm Court Afternoon Tea Guide (2026) — Prices & What to Expect | Hotel Menu Guide"
  description="An independent guide to afternoon tea at The Palm Court in The Plaza, NYC: the experience, reported prices, what to expect, and how to book. Prices reported as of September 2026."
  path="/plaza-palm-court-menu/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  ogImage="/images/palm-court-tea.webp"
>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
    <img
      src="/images/palm-court-tea.webp"
      alt="Illustration of afternoon tea beneath the Palm Court's glass dome"
      class="w-full h-64 sm:h-96 object-cover rounded-2xl mt-8"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />

    <p class="mt-8 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-gold-dark">
      Menu Guide · The Plaza, Fifth Avenue
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold mt-2 leading-tight">
      The Palm Court Afternoon Tea — Guide
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink-soft">By <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Firoz Khan</span> <span class="text-gold-dark">&middot;</span> FK Digital Media</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      Beneath its famous stained-glass dome, the Palm Court at The Plaza has served afternoon tea
      for over a century — finger sandwiches, warm scones with Devonshire cream, tiered pastries,
      and a long tea list, all in one of the most photographed dining rooms in New York. This is our
      independent guide to the experience: what it costs, what to expect, and how to book.
    </p>

    <!-- At a glance -->
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-line bg-white p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="!mt-0 font-display text-xl font-bold">At a glance</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 text-sm">
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Location</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">768 5th Ave, New York, NY 10019 (The Plaza hotel)</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Served</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Daily, 11:00 AM – 4:00 PM</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Style</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Classic afternoon tea; evening light fare &amp; cocktails after 5 PM</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Reservations</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Essential — book via the hotel's official site or OpenTable</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Phone</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">+1 (212) 546-5300</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Dress</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Smart; guests are encouraged to dress for the occasion</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-guide">
      <h2>The experience</h2>
      <p>
        Afternoon tea here follows the traditional three-act structure. It opens with savory
        finger sandwiches — think smoked salmon, cucumber, egg salad, and seasonal variations —
        moves to warm, freshly baked scones served with Devonshire cream, lemon curd and preserves,
        and finishes with a tier of pastries and sweets that changes with the seasons. A broad tea
        list (black, green, herbal, and rooibos blends) accompanies the food, and champagne can be
        added.
      </p>
      <p>
        The room itself is half the attraction: soaring palm trees, mirrored tables, and the
        celebrated glass dome overhead. Service is formal but warm, and the whole affair typically
        runs about two hours. For children, the Eloise Tea — named for the Plaza's most famous
        fictional resident — offers a kid-friendly version of the ritual.
      </p>

      <h2>Reported prices</h2>
      <p>
        Afternoon tea pricing has risen notably over the years, and it varies by season — holiday
        menus (like the winter Holiday Afternoon Tea) command a premium. Based on recent public
        sources, the realistic bands are:
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line my-6 not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-parchment text-left">
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Tea service</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-right">Reported price (per person)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-line bg-white">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Classic afternoon tea</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">~$125</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Champagne afternoon tea</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">~$110–$155</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Most luxurious / premium tea</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">~$235</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Eloise Tea (children)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">~$115 per child</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        For context: a decade ago, published prices were roughly half of today's — older sources
        listed teas around $60–$110. Prices have climbed steadily since, so if you're working from
        an old blog post, double the number before you go. Tax and an automatic service charge sit
        on top of the listed price, so budget accordingly.
      </p>

      <h2>What to expect</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Timing:</strong> tea is served 11 AM to 4 PM daily; the last seating is mid-afternoon. Evenings switch to light fare and cocktails from 5 PM.</li>
        <li><strong>Booking:</strong> reservations are genuinely essential — walk-ins for tea are rarely accommodated. Book days or weeks ahead for weekends and holidays.</li>
        <li><strong>Duration:</strong> plan on about two hours. This is not a quick bite; it's the point of the visit.</li>
        <li><strong>Dietary needs:</strong> vegetarian options are part of the standard offering; for other requirements, contact the restaurant when booking.</li>
        <li><strong>Seasonal menus:</strong> the pastry tier and sandwich selection rotate, and holiday editions (Thanksgiving, Christmas) are the hardest reservations of the year.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Is it worth it?</h2>
      <p>
        Our honest take: you're paying for the room and the ritual as much as the food. Nobody
        leaves hungry, but the scones-and-sandwiches format is what it is — if you want a serious
        meal, book dinner elsewhere. What you can't replicate is tea under that dome. For a special
        occasion, a first-time visitor, or anyone who grew up on Eloise, it delivers exactly what it
        promises. Just go in knowing the price, book ahead, and order the champagne if the day
        deserves it.
      </p>

      <h2>Planning your visit</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Reserve early</strong> via theplazany.com or OpenTable — especially for weekends and the holiday season.</li>
        <li><strong>Confirm the current price</strong> when booking; seasonal menus move the numbers.</li>
        <li><strong>Arrive on time</strong> — seatings are scheduled, and late arrivals compress your two hours.</li>
        <li><strong>Combine it with Fifth Avenue:</strong> the Plaza sits at the southeast corner of Central Park, an easy walk from the park, Rockefeller Center, and the Fifth Avenue shops.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Price disclaimer -->
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-gold/40 bg-gold/10 p-5 text-sm text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      <p class="font-semibold text-ink mb-1">About these prices</p>
      <p>
        Prices above are <strong>reported as of September 2026</strong>, compiled from recent public
        sources and the hotel's published materials. Afternoon tea prices change seasonally and older
        sources list lower figures — always verify the current price with the restaurant when booking.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-ink-soft">
      <a href="/" class="text-gold-dark font-semibold hover:underline">← Back to all guides</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
